WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.179
ED LU: If you find something that's going to
hit the earth, deflecting it is actually

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:08.250
relatively easy. Because if you find it
many years before it hits the earth, say

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.639
10, 20 years before it hits the earth—
remember, the earth is going to go around

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:15.420
the Sun 10 or 20 times, as will that
object. So these things have a long ways

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.630
to travel before they hit, and that means
a tiny, tiny, tiny change in

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.140
their orbit will keep something from
hitting the earth. ZANDER ROSE: You just slow the

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.660
earth down a little bit. LU: Yes. ROSE: There's a great new Chinese movie coming out about this.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.610
LU: But, for those of you who took
physics, if we all jump at the same time, we

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.020
don't change the velocity of the earth,
because we're connected to it. Right? You

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.079
need an external momentum. So what you actually do — ROSE: That's a boring answer.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:43.770
LU: Yeah, the boring answer is to change the
orbits of the asteroid by a tiny amount.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:49.559
And one of the things that we had been
urging since the foundation of our, the

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:53.129
B612 Foundation, is that we human beings
ought to test that technology because we

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:57.270
know how to do it. And in fact, NASA is
now going to do that test. It's

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:02.250
called the DART mission, again, about 2022
I believe it is. We're just going to

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.129
actually run a small spacecraft into an
asteroid. And that is actually

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.840
sufficient in the mass majority of cases to
keep an asteroid from hitting the earth —

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:15.180
provided you have notice. Now back to your

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.189
question. Predicting that
something is going to hit requires you

00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:23.490
be able to calculate
orbits very, very accurately, and know the

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.250
uncertainties in order to calculate what are the chances that this

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.490
thing hits. And I don't want to give the
impression that we're the only ones

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.909
doing it. There are other people that think
of, they calculate these things

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:37.020
and they put these things in big tables. Okay, but think of what the difference is

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.680
between Google Maps, and if somebody
listed for you every restaurant in New

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:47.939
York City in a big table. What's the
difference? And why is, why was Google

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:53.070
Maps so important to commerce, to
things like Lyft and Uber? They all

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:59.399
started by using Google Maps. Yelp. Airbnb.
Because they didn't, the visual

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:04.380
mapping part, the underlying machinery
was built for them and they could just

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.580
build on top of it. And so what we want
to do is allow people to build

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.660
navigation tools for the solar system
without having to do all the heavy

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.600
lifting.
99.99% of the calculation is exactly the

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:28.800
same. It can happen in the background. And
so we want to provide that as a service.

